ip link show

1: lo: <LOOPBACK,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 65536 qdisc noqueue state UNKNOWN mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
    link/loopback 00:00:00:00:00:00 brd 00:00:00:00:00:00
2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 qdisc fq_codel state UP mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 08:00:27:6d:8c:5e brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
3: enp0s8: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> mtu 1500 qdisc noop state DOWN mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 08:00:27:1e:2b:45 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff

Interface Index and Name

1: lo:
2: enp0s3:

- 1 and 2 are interface indices (internal to the kernel)
- lo is the loopback interface
- enp0s3 is a physical (or virtual) network interface

Flags <...>
Common flags:
- UP → Interface is enabled
- LOWER_UP → Physical link is active
- BROADCAST → Supports broadcast
- MULTICAST → Supports multicast traffic
- LOOPBACK → Loopback interface

MTU – Maximum Transmission Unit (packet size limit)
Example: mtu 1500

qdisc – Queueing discipline (how packets are scheduled)
Example: qdisc fq_codel

state – Interface state
Common states: UP, DOWN, UNKNOWN

MAC Address / Link Type

link/ether 08:00:27:6d:8c:5e brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
- link/ether → Ethernet interface
- 08:00:27:6d:8c:5e → MAC address
- brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff → Broadcast address

Using `ip link show`

Look at the flags and state fields:

- LOWER_UP → The physical layer is active (cable plugged in, switch port connected)  
- Absence of LOWER_UP or NO-CARRIER → No physical connection

Examples:

1. 2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> ... state UP  
   - LOWER_UP → Interface is physically connected  
   - UP → Interface is administratively enabled

2. 3: enp0s8: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP> ... state DOWN  
   - No LOWER_UP → Cable not connected or link down

Step 0: Preparation
Before you touch configuration files:
Know your network details
IPv4: IP address, netmask, gateway, DNS
IPv6: IP address/prefix, gateway, DNS
Interface name (find it with ip link show)
Ensure you have root privileges
Most network changes require sudo or root access.
Backup existing configs
sudo cp /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3 /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3.bak
Step 1: Identify the interface
ip link show
Example output:
2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> ...
Here, interface name is enp0s3.
Step 2: Edit the interface file
RHEL stores interface configs in:
/etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-<interface_name>
sudo vi /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3
Step 2a: Static IPv4 configuration
Example:
DEVICE=enp0s3
BOOTPROTO=static
ONBOOT=yes
IPADDR=192.168.1.100
NETMASK=255.255.255.0
GATEWAY=192.168.1.1
DNS1=8.8.8.8
DNS2=8.8.4.4
Explanation:
BOOTPROTO=static → manual IP
ONBOOT=yes → activate at boot
IPADDR & NETMASK → your IPv4
GATEWAY → default route
DNS1 / DNS2 → resolvers
Step 2b: Static IPv6 configuration
Add these lines in the same file:
IPV6INIT=yes
IPV6ADDR=2001:db8:1::100/64
IPV6_DEFAULTGW=2001:db8:1::1
DNS1=2001:4860:4860::8888
DNS2=2001:4860:4860::8844
Explanation:
IPV6INIT=yes → enable IPv6
IPV6ADDR → static IPv6 with prefix length
IPV6_DEFAULTGW → IPv6 gateway
DNS1 / DNS2 → IPv6 DNS servers
⚠️ Make sure IPv6 addresses don’t conflict with existing devices.
Step 3: Enable and restart networking
sudo nmcli connection reload
sudo nmcli connection up enp0s3
Or using the old network service (RHEL 7+ uses NetworkManager by default):
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
Step 4: Verify the configuration
Check IP addresses:
ip addr show enp0s3
Check connectivity:
ping 192.168.1.1      # IPv4 gateway
ping6 2001:db8:1::1   # IPv6 gateway
Check DNS resolution:
ping google.com
ping6 google.com
Check default routes:
ip route show
ip -6 route show
Step 5: Persist and finalize
Configuration is already saved in /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3.
Ensure firewall rules allow necessary traffic.
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ssh
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Document IP assignments for future reference.

1️⃣ Make sure your DHCP server is running
Check the DHCP service on your server:
sudo systemctl status dhcpd
Status should be active (running).
The server config is usually in:
/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf
Make sure the subnet your client is on is included in the DHCP server config:
subnet 192.168.1.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
    range 192.168.1.100 192.168.1.200;
    option routers 192.168.1.1;
    option domain-name-servers 8.8.8.8, 8.8.4.4;
}
2️⃣ Configure the client to use DHCP
On the client RHEL machine:
Option A: Using nmcli (NetworkManager CLI)
Set IPv4 to DHCP:
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv4.method auto
Optionally, enable DHCP for IPv6:
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv6.method auto
Bring the interface up:
sudo nmcli connection up enp0s3
Option B: Using interface config file
Edit the interface file:
sudo vi /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3
Make sure it has:
DEVICE=enp0s3
BOOTPROTO=dhcp
ONBOOT=yes
Restart NetworkManager:
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
3️⃣ Verify DHCP IP
Check which IP the client got from your DHCP server:
ip addr show enp0s3
You should see an IP in the range configured on the DHCP server, e.g., 192.168.1.100–200.
Check the lease:
sudo cat /var/lib/NetworkManager/dhclient-*.lease
4️⃣ Test Connectivity
Ping the default gateway (router):
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
Ping the DHCP server itself:
ping -c 3 <DHCP-server-IP>
Ping an external IP to test Internet access (if allowed):
ping -c 3 8.8.8.8
5️⃣ What Happens Behind the Scenes
The client broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER message.
The DHCP server responds with a DHCPOFFER including IP, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS.
The client requests that IP using DHCPREQUEST.
The server confirms with DHCPACK.
The client configures its interface with the IP and other settings.

Identify your interface
First, find your network interface:
ip link show
Example output:
1: lo: ...
2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 ...
Let’s assume your interface is enp0s3.
2️⃣ Decide your static IP and network details
You need:
Parameter	Example Value	Notes
IP Address	192.168.1.50	Must be unique in your LAN
Subnet Mask	255.255.255.0	Usually /24 for small networks
Gateway	192.168.1.1	Router or internet gateway
DNS	8.8.8.8 (Google)	Needed for domain name resolution
⚠️ Make sure this IP is not in the DHCP pool of any server/router to avoid conflicts.
3️⃣ Configure static IP using NetworkManager (nmcli)
Step 1: Set IPv4 manually
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.50/24
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv4.gateway 192.168.1.1
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv4.dns "8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4"
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv4.method manual
What happens here:
ipv4.addresses → Assigns your static IP and subnet.
ipv4.gateway → Lets your server send packets to the internet.
ipv4.dns → Lets your server resolve domain names like google.com.
ipv4.method manual → Ensures DHCP is not used.
Step 2: Bring the connection up
sudo nmcli connection up enp0s3
4️⃣ Configure static IP using ifcfg file (alternative)
Edit /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3:
DEVICE=enp0s3
BOOTPROTO=static
ONBOOT=yes
IPADDR=192.168.1.50
NETMASK=255.255.255.0
GATEWAY=192.168.1.1
DNS1=8.8.8.8
DNS2=8.8.4.4
Restart NetworkManager:
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
5️⃣ Verify configuration
Check IP and interface status:
ip addr show enp0s3
Check routing:
ip route show
You should see:
192.168.1.0/24 dev enp0s3 proto kernel scope link src 192.168.1.50
default via 192.168.1.1 dev enp0s3
6️⃣ Test connectivity
Ping your gateway:
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
Ping external IP (internet):
ping -c 3 8.8.8.8
Test DNS resolution:
ping -c 3 google.com
✅ If all succeed, your static IP is working with internet access.

1. Installing an RPM package directly
If you have downloaded .rpm files:
sudo dnf install /path/to/package.rpm
dnf will resolve dependencies automatically if possible.
Example:
sudo dnf install ~/Downloads/httpd-2.4.6-97.el8.x86_64.rpm
2. Installing a package from the repository
If you want dnf to fetch the package automatically from configured repos:
sudo dnf install package_name
Example:
sudo dnf install vim
3. Checking if a package is installed
dnf list installed package_name
Example:
dnf list installed httpd
Or to list all installed packages:
dnf list installed
4. Uninstalling (removing) a package
sudo dnf remove package_name
Example:
sudo dnf remove httpd
5. Updating a package
To update a specific package:
sudo dnf update package_name
To update all packages:
sudo dnf update
To check if updates are available without installing:
dnf check-update
6. Checking available packages in repositories
dnf list available
Or for a specific package:
dnf list available package_name
7. Listing all packages in a repository
dnf repository-packages <repoid> list
Example: List packages in the AppStream repo:
dnf repository-packages AppStream list
1. List all enabled repositories
dnf repolist
This shows only enabled repositories.
Output example:
repo id               repo name                                  status
AppStream             Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 AppStream      2,345
BaseOS                Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 BaseOS         1,234
2. List all repositories (enabled + disabled)
dnf repolist all
Output example:
repo id               repo name                                  status
AppStream             Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 AppStream      enabled
BaseOS                Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 BaseOS         enabled
ExtraRepo             Extra repository                           disabled
Status can be enabled or disabled.
3. Check repository details
dnf repoinfo
Gives more detailed info about each repo, including URLs, priorities, and whether it’s enabled.
🔹 Quick Notes
Repository configs are usually in /etc/yum.repos.d/.
If you want to enable or disable a repo, you can use:
sudo dnf config-manager --set-enabled <repoid>
sudo dnf config-manager --set-disabled <repoid>
1. Enable a repo using dnf config-manager
sudo dnf config-manager --set-enabled <repoid>
Replace <repoid> with the repository ID you want to enable.
Example:
sudo dnf config-manager --set-enabled AppStream
After enabling, you can verify it:
dnf repolist
2. Enable a repo temporarily for one command
If you don’t want to permanently enable it, you can do:
sudo dnf --enablerepo=<repoid> install package_name
Example:
sudo dnf --enablerepo=ExtraRepo install vim
This temporarily enables ExtraRepo just for this command.
3. Enable a repo by editing the .repo file
Repo configurations are in /etc/yum.repos.d/.
Open the repo file:
sudo nano /etc/yum.repos.d/ExtraRepo.repo
Find the line:
enabled=0
Change it to:
enabled=1
Save and exit. Now the repo is enabled permanently.
1. Disable using dnf config-manager
sudo dnf config-manager --set-disabled <repoid>
Replace <repoid> with the repository ID you want to disable.
Example:
sudo dnf config-manager --set-disabled AppStream
Verify the change:
dnf repolist
The disabled repo will no longer appear in the list.
2. Disable a repo temporarily for one command
sudo dnf --disablerepo=<repoid> install package_name
This only disables the repo for this single command.
Example:
sudo dnf --disablerepo=ExtraRepo install vim
3. Disable by editing the .repo file
Open the repo file:
sudo nano /etc/yum.repos.d/ExtraRepo.repo
Find the line:
enabled=1
Change it to:
enabled=0
Save and exit. The repo is now permanently disabled.
3. Check package info and version
dnf info <package_name>
Example:
dnf info httpd

1. View the entire system log
sudo journalctl
Shows all logs collected by systemd since boot.
Scroll with ↑/↓, exit with q.
2. View the log for a specific service
sudo journalctl -u <service_name>
Example: Check logs for Apache (httpd):
sudo journalctl -u httpd
3. Show the logs in real time (like tail -f)
sudo journalctl -f
Or for a specific service:
sudo journalctl -u httpd -f
4. Show logs for the current boot only
sudo journalctl -b
Or for the previous boot:
sudo journalctl -b -1
5. Show logs within a time range
sudo journalctl --since "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" --until "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"
Example:
sudo journalctl --since "2026-04-01 08:00" --until "2026-04-01 12:00"
You can also use keywords like "yesterday" or "1 hour ago":
sudo journalctl --since "yesterday"
6. Show only errors or warnings
sudo journalctl -p err       # only errors
sudo journalctl -p warning   # warnings
Priority levels (-p): emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info, debug.
7. Limit the number of lines
sudo journalctl -n 50
Shows the last 50 lines of logs.
Combine with -f for real-time monitoring:
sudo journalctl -u httpd -n 50 -f
8. Persistent logging
By default, logs may be kept only in memory. To keep logs across reboots:
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/journal
sudo systemd-tmpfiles --create --prefix /var/log/journal
sudo systemctl restart systemd-journald

RHEL system uses LVM and the initramfs is deleted, the system won’t boot normally, because the initramfs is responsible for loading the LVM modules and mounting the root filesystem. You can still recover it using a rescue ISO, but the process is a bit different. Here’s a step-by-step guide:

1. Boot From RHEL ISO in Rescue Mode
Insert your RHEL installation ISO (or bootable USB) and choose Troubleshooting → Rescue a Red Hat Enterprise Linux system.
Choose “Skip mounting” when prompted — we will manually activate LVM volumes.
2. Access the Rescue Shell
You will get a bash shell (usually under /mnt/sysimage or just root of the live system).
3. Scan and Activate LVM Volumes
Make sure LVM tools are available (they should be on the rescue ISO):
lvm pvscan       # Scan physical volumes
lvm vgscan       # Scan volume groups
lvm lvscan       # Scan logical volumes
Activate the volume groups:
vgchange -ay
Now all your logical volumes should be available, e.g., /dev/mapper/rhel-root, /dev/mapper/rhel-swap.
4. Mount the Root Filesystem
Find your root logical volume (usually something like /dev/mapper/<VG>-root) using lvscan.
Mount it manually:
mkdir /mnt/sysimage
mount /dev/mapper/rhel-root /mnt/sysimage
If you have a separate /boot partition (non-LVM), mount it as well:
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/sysimage/boot   # Replace /dev/sda1 with your /boot
5. Rebuild the Initramfs

Now that your root is mounted and the LVM is active, rebuild initramfs:

chroot /mnt/sysimage
# Determine the kernel version
ls /boot/vmlinuz-*

# Rebuild initramfs for the kernel (example: 5.14.0-284.el9.x86_64)
dracut -f /boot/initramfs-5.14.0-284.el9.x86_64.img 5.14.0-284.el9.x86_64
-f forces overwrite if a file exists (though in your case it was deleted, so it just creates a new one).
6. Reinstall GRUB (Optional but Recommended)

If you suspect bootloader issues:

grub2-install /dev/sda
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
7. Exit and Reboot
exit          # Exit chroot
umount -R /mnt/sysimage
reboot
Your system should now boot normally with LVM activated.




1. Boot From RHEL ISO in Rescue Mode
Insert your RHEL bootable USB or ISO.
Choose Troubleshooting → Rescue a Red Hat Enterprise Linux system.
You can choose Auto-mount because there’s no LVM to detect. The rescue environment will usually mount your root filesystem automatically under /mnt/sysimage.
If you prefer manual, you can also choose Skip mounting — either way works for standard partitions.
2. Verify Mounted Root

Check if your root partition is mounted:

ls /mnt/sysimage

You should see directories like:

bin  boot  etc  home  lib  usr  var
If you chose Skip mounting, manually mount your partitions:
mkdir /mnt/sysimage
mount /dev/sda2 /mnt/sysimage       # replace /dev/sda2 with your root partition
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/sysimage/boot  # replace /dev/sda1 with /boot if separate
3. Chroot into Your System

Enter your installed system:

chroot /mnt/sysimage

Now your shell behaves as if you booted into your installed system.

4. Rebuild initramfs
Identify your kernel version:
ls /boot/vmlinuz-*

Example output: vmlinuz-5.14.0-284.el9.x86_64

Rebuild initramfs:
dracut -f /boot/initramfs-5.14.0-284.el9.x86_64.img 5.14.0-284.el9.x86_64
-f forces overwriting if needed.
5. Reinstall GRUB (Optional but Safe)

If you suspect bootloader issues:

grub2-install /dev/sda          # replace /dev/sda with your boot disk
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
6. Exit and Reboot
exit                  # exit chroot
umount -R /mnt/sysimage
reboot
